    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I've been having a major issue with my dark chocolate batches lately. Even when I follow my usual process, I'm getting this weird white, dull coating on the surface after it sets—like a fat bloom. Can you explain the science behind why this happens and what I'm doing wrong with my tempering?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The white, dull coating you're observing is called fat bloom, and it typically occurs when the cocoa butter in dark chocolate crystals out of the solid chocolate on the surface. This can happen if the chocolate is exposed to warmer temperatures while setting or if it's stored in conditions with fluctuating temperature and humidity levels. Proper tempering usually prevents this by ensuring your chocolate is correctly crystallized. It's essential to maintain a consistent temperature during the tempering and storage phases to minimize this issue. You might want to consider keeping your chocolate at a cool but stable temperature, and ensure that your storage room or cupboard doesn't get too warm or drafty.",
